I'd give this game 3 stars out of 5, primarily due to melee controls.
Pros:
1) nice graphics;
2) good performance — i5-3570 with GeForce 560Ti can run this game in 1920x1200 at high settings without any problems;
3) a lot of areas to explore and loot;
4) entertaining driving and car combat mechanics.
Cons:
1) incredibly inconveninent melee-related part of gamepad controls: attack is on X, parry is on Y, special actions/moves are on A, evasion is either RB or B (depends on chosen button layout). So expect a LOT of missed hits (too many actions, all of which have to be performed very quickly because enemies are pretty agile and 1-on-1 non-boss fights are extremely rare, are bound to buttons controlled by one finger + no way to change camera angle to have better view on the situtation) unless you have several thumbs on the right hand;
2) some story missions won't advance until you have a specific set of car upgrades even if you have better ones already installed, forcing you to downgrade the car, do what's needed to mark the objective as completed and then put everything back;
3) aiming assistance in sniper rifle mode may cause problems when you try to shoot enemy snipers while hiding behind a hill or rock — the game automatically adjusts the scope to target the center of enemy's torso so bullets are likely to hit your cover unless you see the whole enemy's body (bullets realistically go out of the barrel which is slightly below the scope);
4) volume of sound effects may temporarily decrease after leaving the map screen.

This game has influenced me in the following ways:
1) I wrote a 40 line poem about W. D. Gaster;
2) customized a picture with Undyne and ordered it as a T-Shirt print;
3) listened to the score em, 223 times of Hopes and Dreams, 159 ofMeGaLovania, 134 of Reunited and many others;
4) not to mention it also made me laugh thousand times.
UPD.26.11.17
5) watched tons of Undertale music clips
6) worked out with Undyne ;)

In my long and storied career as a game critic, I’ve played a lot of bad games. Most of them – for example, Shovel Knight or Kingsway – are what I would call “frustratingly bad”; that is, the game’s problems actively make it less fun to play. A few manage to be what I would call “charmingly bad”; that is, the game’s problems actually contribute to its enjoyment. To use movies as an example, the Ghostbusters reboot is frustratingly bad, but The Room is charmingly bad. The original Life is Strange fell into the latter category and, had I reviewed it, I would have probably recommended it. Max’s time powers provided a lot of unintentional humor to the game, and there were plenty of opportunities to make the more obnoxious characters (especially Chloe) suffer. The plot and the game’s environments began to take very interesting and Silent Hill-esque turns near the end, which is the sort of stuff that I like to see games try. You won’t see me writing slashfic involving the game’s minor characters, but if someone badmouthed the game I could see myself tossing a few mean words their way.
Before the Storm is frustratingly bad. The thing that carried Life is Strange through its first few episodes were Max’s aforementioned time powers, which have now been replaced with – this might blow your mind – Chloe looking at her hand. Chloe is apparently so dumb that she needs to write every minor objective that she has on her palm, which results in some very strange situations (


). Very little work was put into this feature, as you can easily tell by looking at the animation for it (
). Haha whoops, wrong link! (
) To compensate for this, Chloe is also capable of backtalk, which is basically just a word association game. Neither of these two features allow for as much potential as the time rewind ability: the stakes for interactions could be much higher since Max could easily rewind herself out of a bad situation. For example, there’s a scene in the junkyard where Chloe and Max have a gun and are shooting things. If you take too long to tell Chloe what to do, she goes rogue and accidentally shoots herself. This is unexpected and hilarious, and it is now impossible to do anything like this in Before the Storm without a “do-over” ability.
Before the Storm functions as a sort of origin story for Chloe, to explain why she’s so ♥♥♥♥ed up during the original Life is Strange. I’d like to think of it conceptually as the MGS5 of the LiS cinematic universe. The problem is that since we already know Chloe and have expectations for how she should act, the game is very restrictive in terms of how you can respond to things. Max was a blank slate and one of the more tolerable characters as a result, but it also allowed for a wide range of reactions to different scenarios. Since Chloe is an ♥♥♥♥♥♥♥, all of her responses have to be some combination of sarcastic, smarmy, or mean. I know that these sorts of games are famous for presenting only the illusion of choice, but Before the Storm skips the illusion and makes it outright clear that this whole show is on rails.
Playing as Chloe comes with other problems: namely, that Chloe is an awful character. Before the Storm starts off strong, with Chloe constantly talking about how much she loves weed and partying. This transitions into her being an ♥♥♥♥♥♥♥ to every person she comes in contact with, until sliding into the final third of the game. This is where things get, heh, strange. The original game clearly drew inspiration from Twin Peaks, so it would naturally follow that Before the Storm would attempt to ape some of the plot points of Fire Walk with Me. The evidence that I provide to support this claim is the fact that the band Chloe sees at the beginning of the game is subtly called “Firewalk”. The problem is that it is immediately obvious that the devs at DONTNOD’s B-team lack the creativity or cajones that Lynch does, so the game has (so far) operated exactly as you would have expected it to. I also don’t want to look ♥♥♥♥♥♥♥♥ here, but I honestly forgot that Rachel was a character in Life is Strange which is either a sign that the game wasn’t very successful at establishing her importance, or that I pushed that part of it completely out of my mind. On the flipside, it’s impossible to imagine Twin Peaks without Laura despite the fact that we basically didn’t even see her until the series had ended.
There is still room for Before the Storm to make the leap from frustrating to charming, but given what I’ve seen and the pedigree behind it I wouldn’t call it likely. There are only two episodes left, and it’s already obvious that most of the last one will involve Rachel getting put into the ground. Chloe will never be a compelling character, and Victoria (the only truly sensible person in the original) appears to have been written as just a total ♥♥♥♥♥. What do I really have to look forward to here, gamers? I can only hope that, once I’ve played the rest of the game, I don’t feel an urge to time rewind back to this very moment and add an extra note that says “IT DOESN’T GET BETTER.”
Taster's score: C-
Note: IT DOESN’T GET BETTER.

